            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet \\(n=3\\).  \nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines’’ means that the six lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered.\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus a non‑sunny line is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nWe construct three distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Vertical line** \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_{1}: x=1\\).  \n   This line contains the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).\n\n2. **Horizontal line** \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_{2}: y=1\\).  \n   This line contains the points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).\n\n   After \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) are placed, the only point of \\(P\\) not yet covered is \\((2,2)\\).\n\n3. **Sunny line** \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_{3}: y=x\\).  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is sunny.  \n   It passes through \\((2,2)\\) (and also through \\((1,1)\\), already covered, which is allowed).\n\nThe three lines are distinct:\n\\[\nx=1\\neq y=1\\neq y=x.\n\\]\n\nAll six points of \\(P\\) belong to at least one of the three lines:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_{1}\\cap\\ell_{2}\\cap\\ell_{3},\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_{1},\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_{2},\\\\\n(1,3)&\\in\\ell_{1},\\\\\n(2,2)&\\in\\ell_{3},\\\\\n(3,1)&\\in\\ell_{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExactly one of the lines (\\(\\ell_{3}\\)) is sunny, while \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) are non‑sunny.  \n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) there exists a configuration of three distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having precisely \\(k=1\\) sunny line. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
