            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{A=(1,1),\\;B=(1,2),\\;C=(1,3),\\;D=(2,1),\\;E=(2,2),\\;F=(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le 4\\) (because \\(n=3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny lines through points of \\(S\\)\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nFor each unordered pair of distinct points of \\(S\\) we compute the slope:\n\n| Pair | Slope |\n|------|-------|\n| \\(AB\\) | \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) |\n| \\(AC\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\(AD\\) | \\(0\\) (horizontal) |\n| \\(AE\\) | \\(1\\) |\n| \\(AF\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\(BC\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\(BD\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\(BE\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\(BF\\) | \\(-\\tfrac12\\) |\n| \\(CD\\) | \\(-2\\) |\n| \\(CE\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\(CF\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\(DE\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\(DF\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\(EF\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n\nOnly the slopes \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the **only sunny pairs** are  \n\n\\[\nAE,\\qquad BF,\\qquad CD .\n\\]\n\nHence any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S\\), and the only possible sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}=AE,\\qquad \\ell_{2}=BF,\\qquad \\ell_{3}=CD .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑sunny lines that can contain points of \\(S\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nAmong such lines, those that contain **at least two** points of \\(S\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\\\\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\\\\n&x+y=3,\\;x+y=4 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(The lines \\(x=3,\\;y=3,\\;x+y=2\\) contain only a single point of \\(S\\) and therefore cannot be the required third line.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume we have three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \nLet the two sunny lines be any two of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).  \nThey together cover four points of \\(S\\); the two points not covered must lie on the third line, which must be non‑sunny and therefore belong to the list (1).\n\nWe examine the three possible choices of sunny lines.\n\n* **Case 1:** Sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1}=AE\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}=BF\\).  \n  Covered points: \\(A,E,B,F\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\(C=(1,3)\\) and \\(D=(2,1)\\).  \n  No line in (1) contains both \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (their \\(x\\)-coordinates differ, their \\(y\\)-coordinates differ, and \\(C+D\\) have sums \\(4\\) and \\(3\\) respectively).  \n  Hence no suitable non‑sunny line exists.\n\n* **Case 2:** Sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1}=AE\\) and \\(\\ell_{3}=CD\\).  \n  Covered points: \\(A,E,C,D\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\(B=(1,2)\\) and \\(F=(3,1)\\).  \n  Again, none of the lines in (1) passes through both \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) (they have different \\(x\\), different \\(y\\), and sums \\(3\\) and \\(4\\)).  \n\n* **Case 3:** Sunny lines \\(\\ell_{2}=BF\\) and \\(\\ell_{3}=CD\\).  \n  Covered points: \\(B,F,C,D\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\(A=(1,1)\\) and \\(E=(2,2)\\).  \n  No line of (1) contains both \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) (different \\(x\\), different \\(y\\), sums \\(2\\) and \\(4\\)).  \n\nIn each case the two uncovered points are not collinear on any non‑sunny line from (1). Consequently it is impossible to complete the covering with a third line while keeping exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nNo selection of three distinct lines can cover all six points of \\(S\\) with exactly two of the lines being sunny. Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the required configuration with \\(k=2\\) does **not exist**. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
