            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{b}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S\\) is the disjoint union \\(S=\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}S_{b}\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|S_{b}| = n+1-b,\\qquad \n\\sum_{b=1}^{n}|S_{b}| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three directions\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line that is **not** horizontal. Then for every \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap S_{b}\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) contained two distinct points \\((a_{1},b)\\) and \\((a_{2},b)\\) of the same level \\(S_{b}\\), those two points would have identical \\(y\\)-coordinates. The unique line through them would be the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(L\\) is not horizontal. ∎\n\nThe same statement holds for lines that are not vertical, and also for lines that are not of slope \\(-1\\); in each case the line can meet a given level \\(S_{b}\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of lines covers all points of \\(S\\) and does **not** contain the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) for some \\(b\\), then \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least \\(|S_{b}|=n+1-b\\) distinct non‑horizontal lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(S_{b}\\) have to be covered by lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) other than \\(y=b\\). By Lemma 1 each such line can contribute at most one point of \\(S_{b}\\). Hence we need at least as many lines as there are points in \\(S_{b}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument  \n\nAssume that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S\\) and that contains at least one sunny line.  \nBecause a sunny line is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\), the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be missing **some** horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (otherwise we would already have \\(n\\) horizontal lines and no room for a sunny one).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{\\,b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid y=b\\notin\\mathcal L\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in B\\) Lemma 2 forces \\(\\mathcal L\\) to contain at least \\(|S_{b}|=n+1-b\\) non‑horizontal lines.  \nSince the lines required for different values of \\(b\\) are distinct (they belong to different levels of \\(S\\)), we obtain the lower bound\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{b\\in B}(n+1-b)\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)\n      \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\), contradicting the hypothesis \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\nTherefore **no horizontal line can be omitted**; i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\\supseteq\\{\\,y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause these \\(n\\) horizontal lines already exhaust the allowed number of lines, \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists exactly of them. All of them are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none is sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence  \n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}=\\{\\,y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) satisfies the covering condition with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
