\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H'=\\{\\,y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  All points of \\(S_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\) belong to one of these lines.  \n  Choose any sunny line through \\((1,n)\\); for definiteness let  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y=2x+(n-2)\n  \\]\n\n  (its slope is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n  The family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L=\\mathcal H'\\cup\\{L\\}\n  \\]\n\n  consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n  Thus a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A counter‑example to the claimed universal bound \\(k\\le 1\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine three lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently all three are sunny.  \nThe six points of \\(S_3\\) are partitioned as  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\subset L_1,\\qquad\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\subset L_2,\\qquad\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\subset L_3,\n\\]\n\nso the three lines together cover the whole set \\(S_3\\).  \nThus for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists, showing that the statement “\\(k\\le 1\\) for all \\(n\\)” is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remarks on the remaining cases\n\nThe two constructions above settle the problem for the smallest possible numbers of sunny lines and exhibit a concrete counter‑example to the previously claimed bound.  \nThe following questions remain open:\n\n* For a given \\(n\\ge4\\), what is the maximal possible number of sunny lines \\(k_{\\max}(n)\\)?\n* Which intermediate values \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le k_{\\max}(n)\\) can be realised?\n\nThese questions appear to require a finer analysis of how many points a sunny line can contain inside the triangular region \\(S_n\\) and how the different directions can be combined.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nI have rigorously proved that\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable;\n* for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is attainable, disproving the universal bound \\(k\\le1\\).\n\nThe complete description of all admissible \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) is still open."
