n\n5. **Impossibility of any other value of \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n   By Lemma\u202f2 a sunny line contains at most three points of \\(S_n\\); a\n   non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points.  Therefore the total\n   number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3k+n(n-k)=n^{2}+k\\; .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}+k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   must hold.  Solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2-n^{2}=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (1) imposes no restriction.  A sharper bound is obtained by\n   observing that a sunny line containing three points must have slope\n   \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  Such a line uses **exactly** three points whose\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates are consecutive.  Consequently two distinct sunny lines\n   of slope \\(-2\\) (or of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) cannot share a point, and a\n   sunny line of slope \\(1\\) can contain at most three points as well.\n   Therefore the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is\n   at most \\(3k\\), with equality only when every sunny line contains three\n   points.\n\n   If \\(k\\le n-1\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\), then at most \\(3k\\le 3(n-1)\\) points are\n   covered by sunny lines.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at\n   most \\(n(n-k)\\) points, so the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3k+n(n-k)=n^{2}+k- (n-3) .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) this quantity is **strictly smaller** than \\(|S_n|=\n   \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) whenever \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Hence no configuration\n   with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   When \\(n\\) is even, the case \\(k=n\\) is also impossible, because a\n   covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines would require each sunny line to contain\n   exactly three points, giving \\(3n\\) points in total, whereas\n   \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\) for every even \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   Summarising the impossibility results:\n\n   * for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) are impossible;\n   * for even \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n   Together with the explicit constructions of Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4 this yields the\n   complete list of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum size of a sunny line)\n\nInside the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S_n)\\) a line whose slope is not\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) meets the boundary in two points that lie on two different\nedges of the triangle.  Consequently it can contain at most three points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Moreover it contains three points **iff** its slope is \\(-2\\) or\n\\(-\\tfrac12\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  Intersecting with the\nedges \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) gives the three intersection points  \n\n\\[\n(1,\\,m+b),\\qquad\\bigl(\\tfrac{1-b}{m},\\,1\\bigr),\\qquad\n\\bigl(\\tfrac{n+1-b}{m+1},\\,\\tfrac{mn+m+b}{m+1}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nAll lattice points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the segment between two of these\npoints.  Because the three edges have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the only way\nthat the segment contains three lattice points is when the step between\nconsecutive lattice points is \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\); this corresponds to\n\\(m=-2\\) or \\(m=-\\tfrac12\\).  In every other case the step is larger and\nthe segment contains at most two lattice points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Covering bound)\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\)\nnon\u2011sunny lines, then the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be\ncovered by \\(\\mathcal F\\) does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n3k+n(n-k)=n^{2}+k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most three points of \\(S_n\\); a\nnon\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points, because the longest such line in the triangle has length \\(n\\).\nSumming these maxima yields (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence of configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the\n  line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  The\n  only uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\).  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x+(n-2)\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny and together with the\n  \\(n-1\\) horizontals forms a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n  \\(S_n\\) with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nAssume \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Define the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i      &: y=-2x+(2m+3+i), && i=1,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{m+i}  &: y=-\\tfrac12x+(m+2+i), && i=1,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{2m+1} &: y=x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are \\(-2\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) or \\(1\\); therefore every line is sunny.\nA direct verification shows that the three families partition the set\n\\(S_n\\) into triples:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i      &\\;=\\;\\{(i,\\,2m+2-i),\\;(i+1,\\,2m-i),\\;(i+2,\\,2m-1-i)\\},\\\\\nL_{m+i}  &\\;=\\;\\{(i,\\,m+2+i),\\;(i+1,\\,m+1+i),\\;(i+2,\\,m+i)\\},\\\\\nL_{2m+1} &\\;=\\;\\{(1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,(m+1,m+1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) lines are distinct, each contains exactly three points of\n\\(S_n\\), and together they cover the whole set.  Hence for every odd\n\\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of any other value of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n*If \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contains at most three points, and it contains three\npoints only when its slope is \\(-2\\) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  Two distinct lines\nwith slope \\(-2\\) (or with slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) cannot share a point,\nbecause the three points on such a line have consecutive \\(x\\)-coordinates.\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(3k\\) points,\nand equality can hold only when **every** sunny line has slope \\(-2\\) or\n\\(-\\tfrac12\\).\n\nThe non\u2011sunny lines are horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); each of\nthem contains at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence, using Lemma\u202f2, the total number\nof points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n3k+n(n-k)=n^{2}+k-(n-3).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) and \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}+k-(n-3)\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nbecause the left\u2011hand side equals \\(n^{2}-n+3+k\\) and\n\\(n^{2}-n+3+k\\le n^{2}-n+3+(n-1)=n^{2}+2<n^{2}+n\\) while\n\\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2=n^{2}/2+n/2\\) exceeds the former for every\n\\(n\\ge4\\).  Thus (3) is insufficient to cover all points, a contradiction.\nHence no configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n\\) and \\(n\\) is even.*  \nWhen \\(k=n\\) all lines are sunny, so each must contain at most three points.\nTherefore the total number of points covered is at most \\(3n\\).\nFor even \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n3n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nbecause the inequality is equivalent to \\(6< n+1\\), which holds.\nHence a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility statements we obtain:\n\n* for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) are impossible;\n* for even \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final classification\n\nFrom Sections\u202f1,\u202f2 and\u202f3 we conclude that the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{\\,n\\mid n\\text{ odd}\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  No other non\u2011negative integer can occur. \u220e"

