ope \\(-2\\)).\n   Remove \\(P_1,P_2\\) from \\(S_m\\).  \n   The remaining points consist of  \n\n   * the triangle \\(S_{m-2}\\) (all points with \\(a+b\\le m\\)), and  \n   * the vertical column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le m-1\\}\\) of size \\(m-2\\).\n\n   By the induction hypothesis \\(S_{m-2}\\) can be covered with \\((m-3)\\)\n   sunny lines.  The vertical column is covered by the single sunny line\n   \\(L_1:\\;y=x+1\\) (slope \\(1\\)), which contains the points\n   \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(m-2,m-1)\\).  \n\n   Altogether we have the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0,\\;L_1,\\;\\text{the }(m-3)\\text{ lines from the induction step},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(1+(m-3)+1=m-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(S_m\\).\n   ∎\n\n4. **Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Take the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second\n   coordinate is at most \\(n-1-k\\).  \n   The uncovered points form the set \\(S_{k+1}\\) (the right‑angled triangle\n   of size \\(k+1\\)).  By Lemma 2, \\(S_{k+1}\\) can be covered by exactly\n   \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  Adding those \\(k\\) sunny lines to the\n   \\(n-1-k\\) horizontals yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with\n   precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Existence of \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2t+1\\) be odd.  \n   Lemma 2 gives a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n   Choose any point of \\(S_n\\) (for instance \\((1,1)\\)) and add a new sunny\n   line through that point that is different from all previously used\n   lines (e.g. the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\((1,1)\\)).  The added line\n   does not destroy the covering, and now we have \\(n\\) distinct sunny\n   lines.  Thus \\(k=n\\) is possible for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of any other \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, while a non‑sunny line\n   contributes at most \\(n\\) points (the longest horizontal, vertical or\n   slope \\(-1\\) line inside the triangle).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2t\\).  Then\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=t\\) and (2) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kt+(n-k)n=n^2-kt .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives \\(kt\\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).\n   Because \\(t=n/2\\), this inequality forces \\(k\\le n-1\\).  Consequently\n   \\(k=n\\) cannot occur when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2t+1\\).  Then\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=t+1\\) and (2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k(t+1)+(n-k)n=n^2-\\!k t .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality is satisfied for every \\(k\\le n\\); however, if\n   \\(k=n-1\\) the right‑hand side equals \\(n^2-(n-1)t=n^2- t n + t\\),\n   which is still at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Hence the bound does not\n   forbid any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  The constructions of Sections 4\n   and 5 show that all these values indeed occur, while the argument above\n   shows that no value \\(k>n\\) is possible (there are only \\(n\\) lines).\n\n   Combining the two cases we obtain the complete list of admissible\n   values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nLet a sunny line have slope \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) written in\nlowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap S_n\\bigr)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|q|}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  If \\((x_0,y_0)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), all other lattice points of\n\\(L\\) are \\((x_0+qk,\\;y_0+pk)\\) with \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Hence the\n\\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(L\\cap S_n\\) form an arithmetic progression\nwith step \\(|q|\\).  Because \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|q|}\\big\\rfloor+1\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|q|}\\big\\rfloor\\)\npoints can appear.  For a sunny line we have \\(|q|\\ge 2\\) (otherwise the\nslope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)), which gives the second inequality\nin (1). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 2\\) there exist \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines that\ncover all points of \\(S_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=2\\).*  \\(S_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\); any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) works.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for \\(m-2\\) (\\(m\\ge4\\)).\nConsider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,m),\\qquad P_2=(2,m-2).\n\\]\n\nThey lie on the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;y=-2x+m+2\\qquad(\\text{slope }-2).\n\\]\n\nDelete \\(P_1,P_2\\) from \\(S_m\\).  The remaining set splits into\n\n* the triangle \\(S_{m-2}\\) (all points with \\(a+b\\le m\\)), and  \n* the vertical column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le m-1\\}\\) of size \\(m-2\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(S_{m-2}\\) can be covered by \\(m-3\\) sunny\nlines.  The vertical column is covered by the single sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\n\\]\n\nwhich contains the points \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(m-2,m-1)\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_0,L_1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{the }(m-3)\\text{ lines from the induction step}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(1+(m-3)+1=m-1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point\nof \\(S_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)).  \nTake the horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate does not\nexceed \\(n-1-k\\).  The uncovered points are exactly the set \\(S_{k+1}\\)\n(the right‑angled triangle of size \\(k+1\\)).  By Lemma 2, \\(S_{k+1}\\) can\nbe covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  Adding those \\(k\\) sunny lines to\nthe \\(n-1-k\\) horizontals yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\nn-1\\) is realizable.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, Lemma 1 gives that each line contains at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor = \\frac{n}{2}\\) points.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |S_n|\n\\qquad (n\\ge 4),\n\\]\n\nso the points of \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) is\nimpossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  \nWrite \\(n=2t+1\\).  Lemma 2 provides a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n-1\\) sunny\nlines.  Choose any point of \\(S_n\\) (e.g. \\((1,1)\\)) and add a new sunny\nline through that point that is different from all previously used lines\n(e.g. the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\((1,1)\\)).  The added line does not\nremove any covered point, and now we have \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that\nstill cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable for every\nodd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No other values of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, while a non‑sunny line\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points (the longest horizontal, vertical or slope\n\\(-1\\) line inside the triangle).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |S_n|\n   \\le k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor + (n-k)n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(n=2t\\) and \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=t\\).\nEquation (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le kt + n^{2}-kn = n^{2}-kt .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives \\(kt\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\), i.e.\n\\(k\\le n-1\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, \\(n=2t+1\\) and \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=t+1\\).\nThen (2) reads  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le k(t+1)+n^{2}-kn = n^{2}-kt .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(kt\\ge 0\\), the right‑hand side is at least \\(n^{2}-t n =\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\); thus (2) imposes no restriction beyond \\(k\\le n\\).\nThe constructions of Sections 1 and 2 show that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (the case \\(k=n\\) only when \\(n\\) is odd) does\nindeed occur.  No larger \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\)\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final classification\n\nCombining the existence results (Sections 1 and 2) with the impossibility\nargument (Section 3) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\quad\\text{for every }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nand, in addition,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=n\\ \\text{when }n\\text{ is odd}. \\;}\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer can be the number of sunny lines in a\nconfiguration satisfying the conditions of the problem. ∎"
