n step}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(1+(m-3)+1=m-1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point\nof \\(S_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)).  \nTake the horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate does not\nexceed \\(n-1-k\\).  The uncovered points are exactly the set \\(S_{k+1}\\)\n(the right‑angled triangle of size \\(k+1\\)).  By Lemma 2, \\(S_{k+1}\\) can\nbe covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  Adding those \\(k\\) sunny lines to\nthe \\(n-1-k\\) horizontals yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\nn-1\\) is realizable.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, Lemma 1 gives that each line contains at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor = \\frac{n}{2}\\) points.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |S_n|\n\\qquad (n\\ge 4),\n\\]\n\nso the points of \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) is\nimpossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  \nWrite \\(n=2t+1\\).  Lemma 2 provides a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n-1\\) sunny\nlines.  Choose any point of \\(S_n\\) (e.g. \\((1,1)\\)) and add a new sunny\nline through that point that is different from all previously used lines\n(e.g. the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\((1,1)\\)).  The added line does not\nremove any covered point, and now we have \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that\nstill cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable for every\nodd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No other values of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration w