\\end{cases}}\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, (3) would give  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le n\\cdot M_n=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n\\cdot\\dfrac{n}{2}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}2<n\\cdot\\dfrac{n+1}{2}=|S_n|, & n\\text{ even},\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   a contradiction.  Hence for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n   *Lines of slope \\(+1\\):* for \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,m\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   A_t:\\;y=x+t .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Lines of slope \\(-2\\):* for \\(s=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   B_s:\\;y=-2x+(2m+2+s) .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in[-m-1,m]\\).  \n   If \\(b-a\\ge0\\) then \\(b-a=t\\) for a unique \\(t\\in[0,m]\\) and the point lies on \\(A_t\\).  \n   If \\(b-a\\le -m-1\\) then \\(b+2a=2m+2+s\\) for a unique \\(s\\in[1,m]\\) and the point lies on \\(B_s\\).  \n   Thus the \\(m+1+m=n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\); all of them are sunny because their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), neither of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Use the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\;y=i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   They are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate \\(b\\le n-1-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the uncovered set.*  \n   After removing the points covered by (6) the remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;b\\ge n-k\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\).  \n   The inequalities become  \n\n   \\[\n   a',b'\\ge 0,\\qquad a'+b'\\le k+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{(k+1)(k+2)}2\\); geometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled triangle congruent to \\(S_{k+1}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – covering \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Set \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   – **Lines of slope \\(+1\\).**  \n     For \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     C_t:\\;y=x+t .\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n   – **Lines of slope \\(-2\\).**  \n     For \\(s=1,2,\\dots ,k-m\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     D_s:\\;y=-2x+(k+2+s) .\n     \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is \\((m)+(k-m)=k\\); all have slopes \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\), therefore they are sunny.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(d\\ge 0\\).*  \n   Because of (8) we have \\(d\\le m-1\\). Indeed,\n   \\[\n   d=b-a\\le (k+1-a)-a = k+1-2a\\le k-1,\n   \\]\n   and the inequality \\(a\\ge n-k\\ge 1\\) forces \\(d\\le m-1\\).  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(C_d\\) (since \\(b=a+d\\)).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(d\\le -m\\).*  \n   Write \\(d=-m-q\\) with \\(q\\ge0\\). From (8) we obtain\n   \\[\n   b+2a = (a+d)+2a = 3a+d = 3a-m-q .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\le k+1\\) (by (8)), the quantity \\(b+2a\\) ranges exactly over the integers\n   \\[\n   k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,k+2+(k-m) .\n   \\]\n   Hence there is a unique \\(s\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k-m\\}\\) such that \\(b+2a=k+2+s\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (10) and lies on \\(D_s\\).\n\n   The two cases exhaust all possibilities, so every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\{C_t\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\}\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – the whole family.*  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-1-k}\\}\\cup\\{C_0,\\dots ,C_{m-1},D_1,\\dots ,D_{k-m}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by the discussion above every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists (the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) above).  \n   * If \\(n\\) is odd, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists (the family (4)–(5)).  \n   * If \\(n\\) is even, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible by the bound (3).  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,n\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}\\,\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
